# ReScaleFrame settings.
#
# Copy this beside the proxy, as ReScaleFrame.ini next to Ace7Game.exe, and edit it there. It is
# read once when the proxy attaches. One NAME=value per line; # or ; starts a comment.
#
# These are the same names that used to be Steam launch options, and an environment variable still
# wins over the file, so an existing launch line keeps working and a one-off override does not mean
# editing this.
#
# Numbers accept hexadecimal with an 0x prefix, and zero means zero rather than "unset".

# Watch the game's D3D11 use. Nothing else works without it.
RSF_OBSERVE=1

# Run the engine's own temporal jitter, which every super resolution backend needs. Off in the
# game's menu; this steps over the gate rather than turning temporal AA on.
RSF_ENABLE_JITTER=1

# Render scale, as a percentage. The panel can change this at runtime.
RSF_SCREEN_PERCENTAGE=50

# 0 Native, 1 Quality, 2 Balanced, 3 Performance, 4 Ultra Performance.
RSF_DLSS_QUALITY=3

# Where dumps, logs and captures are written. A Windows path as Proton sees it.
RSF_DUMP_DIR=Z:\home\n1ght\Projekte\ReScaleFrame\.local\observe

# Left unset on purpose: the vendor runtime is found at ReScaleFrame\streamline beside the proxy.
# RSF_STREAMLINE_BIN=

# Frame capture, off unless asked for, and not a default worth having. RenderDoc wraps the device,
# NGX then refuses to create the DLSS feature, and the retry costs the game a little more every
# frame until it stops. Turn it on for a capture session, off for anything else. When on,
# renderdoc.dll is found beside the proxy; the path is only for a copy kept elsewhere.
# RSF_RENDERDOC=1
# RSF_RENDERDOC_DLL=

# Let translucent geometry write motion vectors. Stock 4.18 excludes it, which is why the mission
# map's relief and the vehicle symbols in replay have none. Untested against the game.
# RSF_TRANSLUCENT_VELOCITY=1

# Separate translucency resolution.
#
# 4.18 renders that layer at half the scene and doubles it back on composite, so at a 50% render
# scale the mission map's relief is a quarter resolution image by the time anything sees it. The
# proxy patches the halving out; these decide what it renders at instead.
#
# Both targets are percentages of the presented resolution, not of the scene, because the scene
# moves: DLSS, XeSS and FSR each pick a different render scale per quality level, and a fixed
# multiplier would mean a different translucency resolution for every one of them. The multiplier
# is derived from whichever render scale is in effect.
#
# 0 means "match the scene", which is the ordinary case and costs nothing over the current
# behaviour. The heavy target applies to a frame whose translucent layer is carrying scene
# geometry rather than a few effects, which in practice is the mission briefing: its relief is the
# scene and reconstructing it from half of a half is what made it look unupscaled. 100 there draws
# it at native however low the render scale is set.
# RSF_TRANSLUCENCY_TARGET=0
# RSF_TRANSLUCENCY_TARGET_HEAVY=100

# What counts as heavy, in indices drawn into the layer in one frame. The captured briefing is
# 540,030 across 59 draws; gameplay effects are orders of magnitude below that. The log names the
# count each time it crosses, so this can be set from what the game draws rather than guessed.
# RSF_TRANSLUCENCY_HEAVY_INDICES=100000

# A direct multiplier in percent, overriding both targets when it is not zero. For pinning the
# layer to one resolution regardless of render scale.
# RSF_TRANSLUCENCY_SCALE=0

# Going above the scene's resolution needs the engine to build a depth to match, which stock 4.18
# does only below 1.0: at or above it the layer borrows the scene's depth, and a large colour target
# with a small depth is a pair D3D11 rejects. Game-tested, that made the briefing relief vanish
# entirely. The proxy narrows that borrow to exactly 1.0 with four one-byte patches, after which the
# engine allocates, fills, view-transforms and resolves the layer's own depth at any size. There is
# no setting for it: if those patches do not match, the scale holds at the scene's resolution by
# itself and says so in the log.
